<main class="irm-page">
        <div class="irm-page-layout">
            <aside class="irm-sidebar">
                <%- include('../../partials/sidebar-nav', { module: 'sam', moduleLabel: 'SAM', activePage: typeof activePage !== 'undefined' ? activePage : '' }) %>
                
                <div class="sidebar-section toc-section">
                    <div class="sidebar-label">On This Page</div>
                    <nav class="sidebar-nav">
                        <a href="#content-library" class="toc-link">The Content Library</a><a href="#status" class="toc-link">Normalization Statuses</a><a href="#rules" class="toc-link">Normalization Rules</a>
                    </nav>
                </div>
            </aside>

            <div class="irm-content">
                
                <div class="content-header">
                    <div class="breadcrumb">
                        <a href="/">Home</a><span class="breadcrumb-sep">›</span><a href="/sam">SAM</a><span class="breadcrumb-sep">›</span><span>Discovery & Normalization</span>
                    </div>
                    <h1 class="content-title"><span class="gradient-text">Normalization</span></h1>
                    <p class="content-subtitle">
                        Transforming messy, inconsistent raw strings into standard Publisher, Product, and Version names to make reconciliation mathematically possible.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <section class="content-section" id="content-library">
                    <h2>The Central Content Library</h2>
                    <p>
                        ServiceNow maintains a massive, cloud-based Content Library containing millions of Publisher Part Numbers (PPNs), publisher names, and product lifecycles. 
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        When raw data is ingested into your instance, the Normalization Engine compares it against this library. By opting into the <strong>Content Service</strong>, instances securely send unrecognized strings to ServiceNow's research team, who continually add them to the global library.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section class="content-section" id="status">
                    <h2>Normalization Statuses</h2>
                    <p>Every Discovery Model evaluates to a specific Normalization Status:</p>
                    
                    <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Normalized</div>
                        <p>A perfect match was found. Publisher, Product, and Version were definitively identified based on library rules.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--warning">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Partially Normalized</div>
                        <p>Publisher and Product were identified, but the exact Version or Edition could not be determined. This often requires manual intervention.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--warning">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Publisher Normalized</div>
                        <p>Only the Publisher (e.g., "Microsoft") was recognized. The product itself remains unknown to the library.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--danger">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Match Not Found</div>
                        <p>The raw string is entirely unrecognized. This is common for proprietary, in-house developed software. You must create custom Normalization Rules for these.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="content-section" id="rules">
                    <h2>Custom Normalization Rules</h2>
                    <p>
                        For software not found in the Content Library, SAM administrators can create custom rules. 
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Exact Match:</strong> Requires the raw string to match the specified condition exactly.</li>
                        <li><strong>Pattern Match:</strong> Uses Regular Expressions (Regex) or "Starts With/Contains" logic. For example, any software containing "InHouse CRM v*" is automatically normalized to the custom Publisher "MyCorp".</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
        
            </div>
        </div>
    </main>

